1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
There were floating cities thunder into the deep blue sea.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
They represent centuries of mechanical evolution.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
And they hold back the tide to build the biggest machines on the planet.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Now, Shipyards on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Shipyards.

6
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:30,900
They're giant waterside construction sites,

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,400
where the extraordinary take shape,

8
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:36,300
places where the largest of machines

9
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:39,440
are constructed, retrofitted, and repaired.

10
00:00:44,140 --> 00:00:46,560
In times of peace, they create the vessels that carry

11
00:00:46,660 --> 00:00:49,760
commerce and international trade.

12
00:00:49,860 --> 00:00:52,560
And during war, they build the mightiest weapons

13
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:54,000
the world has ever known.

14
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,000
On the shores of the Chesapeake Bay sits one of the United

15
00:01:02,100 --> 00:01:04,900
States' largest and oldest shipyards.

16
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,740
The sprawling 500-acre Northrop Grumman Newport News Facility

17
00:01:09,840 --> 00:01:12,740
is situated on nearly two miles of waterfront.

18
00:01:18,360 --> 00:01:21,160
Workers at the facility are responsible for building and

19
00:01:21,260 --> 00:01:25,160
maintaining US aircraft carriers, the most complicated warships

20
00:01:25,161 --> 00:01:28,360
ships to ever rule the waves.

21
00:01:28,460 --> 00:01:30,900
When we bring people into the shipyard, invariably,

22
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:32,100
that's the first thing.

23
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:33,900
When you look at an aircraft carrier, they walk up to it.

24
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,600
And it's hard to conceptualize that what they're looking at

25
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:42,620
is a ship because it takes up the entire horizon.

26
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:45,880
And when you think about putting that ship together

27
00:01:45,980 --> 00:01:48,780
and starting with a plate of steel or that piece of pipe,

28
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:51,660
it is an awesome challenge.

29
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:53,760
And it is a deep sense of pride when

30
00:01:53,761 --> 00:01:55,560
when you get to the finish line.

31
00:01:55,660 --> 00:01:59,060
Carrier construction can take nearly eight years

32
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:00,860
and relies on nearly every muscle

33
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:02,360
that the shipyard has to offer.

34
00:02:11,220 --> 00:02:13,720
Because multi-ton ship parts can't be picked up

35
00:02:13,820 --> 00:02:15,820
at the local home improvement center,

36
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:20,420
shipyards have to build everything they can't buy.

37
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:22,820
The shipyard is a huge place.

38
00:02:22,821 --> 00:02:24,980
At one point in time, we had the capacity

39
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:28,160
to do everything that was in the ship.

40
00:02:28,260 --> 00:02:30,820
Over the years, we've become more specialized.

41
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:34,860
We've retained certain core capabilities in the shipyard.

42
00:02:34,960 --> 00:02:36,360
One example is our foundry.

43
00:02:36,361 --> 00:02:38,360
Foundry.

44
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:55,440
Workers within the over 250,000 square foot

45
00:02:55,540 --> 00:02:59,720
foundry pour up to 2,900 degree Fahrenheit molten metal

46
00:02:59,820 --> 00:03:06,360
into castings to form many of the ship's unique pieces.

47
00:03:06,361 --> 00:03:09,400
We also have other shops that make up the individual

48
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:10,500
components.

49
00:03:10,600 --> 00:03:12,600
We have electrical component shops.

50
00:03:12,700 --> 00:03:14,860
We have a pipe shop.

51
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:17,500
We have a machine shop capable of machining anything you would

52
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:20,500
ever want to machine.

53
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:23,080
All these shops are equipped with the state-of-the-art

54
00:03:23,180 --> 00:03:23,980
equipment.

55
00:03:26,540 --> 00:03:29,480
But all these shops could be part of any huge industrial

56
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:30,880
work site.

57
00:03:30,980 --> 00:03:34,120
What sets shipyards apart is the size of the material being

58
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:35,180
created.

59
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:39,400
And the fact that the final product must eventually find

60
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:40,600
its way to water.

61
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:46,340
Enormous dry docks are how many shipyards

62
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:49,540
deal with both problems.

63
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:52,200
I'm standing on top of the intermediate gate

64
00:03:52,300 --> 00:03:55,340
in the largest dry dock in the Western Hemisphere.

65
00:03:55,440 --> 00:03:58,020
Here at Northrop Grumman, Newport News,

66
00:03:58,120 --> 00:04:00,080
the purpose of this intermediate gate

67
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:02,860
gives us the option to divide this dry dock up

68
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:04,480
into two sections.

69
00:04:04,481 --> 00:04:12,480
It's 2,173 feet long and 250 foot wide with a depth of about 40 feet.

70
00:04:12,481 --> 00:04:15,920
What you see here is just half of this massive dry dock.

71
00:04:15,921 --> 00:04:17,920
The other half is behind me.

72
00:04:17,921 --> 00:04:23,040
A dry dock is a concrete berth with a watertight gate at one end.

73
00:04:23,041 --> 00:04:29,440
Inside these often massive enclosures, shipyard workers both repair and build vessels.

74
00:04:29,441 --> 00:04:33,440
modules. During ship construction, the

75
00:04:33,441 --> 00:04:36,640
dry dock is the focal point of the yard, where towering

76
00:04:36,641 --> 00:04:41,180
preassembled modules are joined together.

77
00:04:41,181 --> 00:04:43,880
But as with all modern shipyards, the building

78
00:04:43,881 --> 00:04:47,260
process starts small. The construction of the

79
00:04:47,261 --> 00:04:50,500
components for the carrier start in the steel fab

80
00:04:50,501 --> 00:04:56,640
facility, where we built small pieces, bulkheads, girders,

81
00:04:56,641 --> 00:05:00,480
foundations that come together to make up preassemblies.

82
00:05:04,540 --> 00:05:07,480
Those bigger preassemblies are then brought together in another

83
00:05:07,580 --> 00:05:10,480
shop to make subassemblies, which are a little bigger.

84
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:18,400
These can get up to 50 to 150 tons.

85
00:05:18,401 --> 00:05:25,000
over the course of months, workers weld, fit, and connect

86
00:05:25,100 --> 00:05:29,940
multiple sub-assemblies into even bigger modules,

87
00:05:30,040 --> 00:05:35,160
the largest of which are called superlifts and can weigh in at a

88
00:05:35,260 --> 00:05:40,760
staggering 900 tons. To move these enormous ship elements,

89
00:05:40,860 --> 00:05:44,300
workers make use of one of the shipyard's most conspicuous

90
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:48,400
pieces of equipment.

91
00:05:48,401 --> 00:05:52,400
a crane. But like everything else in the shipyard,

92
00:05:52,401 --> 00:05:56,400
these construction tools are on a supermassive scale.

93
00:05:56,401 --> 00:05:59,400
At the far end of the shipyard, there's a crane.

94
00:05:59,401 --> 00:06:02,400
We call it the 900-ton crane, and we call it the 900-ton crane

95
00:06:02,401 --> 00:06:05,400
because that's the capacity for a lift.

96
00:06:05,401 --> 00:06:08,400
And you say, well, what other business would require such a

97
00:06:08,401 --> 00:06:10,400
crane? And there probably aren't any.

98
00:06:10,401 --> 00:06:13,400
Those kinds of things are unique to shipyards.

99
00:06:13,401 --> 00:06:19,400
The crane's 540 feet wide.

100
00:06:19,401 --> 00:06:21,400
It's 290 feet tall.

101
00:06:21,401 --> 00:06:25,400
It weighs approximately a little over 10 million pounds.

102
00:06:25,401 --> 00:06:28,400
It has 37 miles of electrical cable.

103
00:06:28,401 --> 00:06:34,400
At full power, it uses enough power to supply 114 houses.

104
00:06:34,401 --> 00:06:37,400
People don't really understand how big the crane is.

105
00:06:37,401 --> 00:06:40,400
The letters on the crane are three stories tall,

106
00:06:40,401 --> 00:06:43,300
And it takes 45 gallons of paint to paint them.

107
00:06:47,340 --> 00:06:49,780
The crane operator sits over 200 feet

108
00:06:49,880 --> 00:06:54,240
above the ground, while crews below attach the massive cables

109
00:06:54,340 --> 00:06:55,240
to the superlifts.

110
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:03,020
Gantry cranes like this one are ideal for shipyard construction

111
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:05,620
because they carry heavier loads which hang directly

112
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:09,660
below the structure, as opposed to boom cranes,

113
00:07:09,661 --> 00:07:14,160
which hang the loads from the end of an arm.

114
00:07:14,260 --> 00:07:16,960
Gantry cranes also allow the loads to be moved great

115
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:21,900
distances due to their rolling base, a feature that allows the

116
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:27,520
superlift to be placed anywhere inside the massive dry dock.

117
00:07:27,620 --> 00:07:30,920
But in spite of the gigantic tools, the shipyard's greatest

118
00:07:31,020 --> 00:07:33,820
attribute remains the craftspeople that have learned

119
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:37,160
the art and skill of making ships.

120
00:07:37,161 --> 00:07:39,660
It's a large number of folks.

121
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:41,460
Right now, at Northrop Grumman-Newport News,

122
00:07:41,560 --> 00:07:43,700
we've got about 18,000 employees.

123
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:45,660
It takes a lot of time and a lot of talent

124
00:07:45,760 --> 00:07:47,300
to bring all this together.

125
00:07:47,400 --> 00:07:50,700
And this is something that's unique to our industry,

126
00:07:50,800 --> 00:07:53,480
especially when you consider the size of the product

127
00:07:53,580 --> 00:07:55,120
that we're producing.

128
00:07:55,220 --> 00:07:57,640
The vessels the craftspeople create

129
00:07:57,740 --> 00:08:00,720
are the end product of a long evolutionary process

130
00:08:00,820 --> 00:08:03,520
spurred through the ages by revolutions in the shipyard.

131
00:08:03,521 --> 00:08:08,520
For ancient civilizations, shipyards like these didn't

132
00:08:08,620 --> 00:08:09,520
exist.

133
00:08:12,520 --> 00:08:16,060
And scientists who look for these early construction sites

134
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:18,160
engage in a frustrating task.

135
00:08:18,260 --> 00:08:19,460
We know they're supposed to be there.

136
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:21,480
We haven't found one yet, or at least we

137
00:08:21,580 --> 00:08:23,180
don't think we found one.

138
00:08:23,280 --> 00:08:27,380
So we're waiting for the next great discovery, the breakthrough.

139
00:08:27,480 --> 00:08:28,780
Archaeologists are incredible optimists,

140
00:08:28,880 --> 00:08:31,220
and every time I go on the field, I hope this is the time

141
00:08:31,320 --> 00:08:32,920
I'd like to find one.

142
00:08:32,921 --> 00:08:35,820
A good number of the ancient sites that we're interested in,

143
00:08:35,920 --> 00:08:37,500
where we know there were shipyards,

144
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:39,720
are now still functioning as harbors.

145
00:08:39,820 --> 00:08:42,760
So you've got the modern overlaying the ancient.

146
00:08:42,860 --> 00:08:44,760
And the second thing is, they were never

147
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:46,240
very major installations.

148
00:08:46,340 --> 00:08:48,400
Ships in antiquity were built on the beach,

149
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:51,500
simply propping up a hull that was going to be worked on.

150
00:08:51,600 --> 00:08:54,980
That's still being done in places like Turkey and Greece today.

151
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:57,320
And that's what would have been done in antiquity.

152
00:08:57,420 --> 00:09:00,680
But some evidence of early shipyards does exist.

153
00:09:00,681 --> 00:09:01,780
exist.

154
00:09:01,781 --> 00:09:03,780
For the Egyptians, clues to theirs

155
00:09:03,781 --> 00:09:06,820
are appropriately buried with their dead.

156
00:09:06,821 --> 00:09:09,060
We're very fortunate to have a number of depictions

157
00:09:09,061 --> 00:09:13,660
of boats and ships being built, presumably along the Nile,

158
00:09:13,661 --> 00:09:16,740
presumably in shipyards by definition.

159
00:09:16,741 --> 00:09:19,060
There's one painted relief in a tomb

160
00:09:19,061 --> 00:09:21,200
belonging to a guy by the name of T

161
00:09:21,201 --> 00:09:24,700
that dates to the latter part of the third millennium BC.

162
00:09:24,701 --> 00:09:27,700
You have people lobbing the branches off logs.

163
00:09:27,701 --> 00:09:30,480
Then you have planks with people sitting on top of them,

164
00:09:30,481 --> 00:09:33,380
and cutting mortars and tenon joints into the planks.

165
00:09:33,381 --> 00:09:36,820
And they would do this with a chisel and a mallet.

166
00:09:36,821 --> 00:09:41,260
Throughout the Greek and Roman empires, the Middle Ages,

167
00:09:41,261 --> 00:09:45,000
and into 15th century China, where massive 400-foot long

168
00:09:45,001 --> 00:09:48,060
wooden ships were constructed, design and vessel size

169
00:09:48,061 --> 00:09:49,900
were altered greatly.

170
00:09:49,901 --> 00:09:53,440
But the yards, techniques, and tools used to build them

171
00:09:53,441 --> 00:09:55,340
remained little changed.

172
00:09:55,341 --> 00:09:57,900
For Italian shipwrights and European master carpenters

173
00:09:57,901 --> 00:10:00,700
of the Renaissance, the two most important attributes

174
00:10:00,701 --> 00:10:04,120
of a shipyard were a gently sloped piece of land

175
00:10:04,121 --> 00:10:06,180
and a deep water channel.

176
00:10:06,181 --> 00:10:09,980
In the yard, workers used a chopping tool known as an adze

177
00:10:09,981 --> 00:10:12,940
to hue and shape the internal timber frames,

178
00:10:12,941 --> 00:10:16,140
while the mechanical advantage provided by block and tackle,

179
00:10:16,141 --> 00:10:18,680
coupled with animal power, helped shipbuilders

180
00:10:18,681 --> 00:10:22,060
to lift heavy masts and yard arms into place.

181
00:10:22,061 --> 00:10:23,560
The hammers were there, the chisels were there,

182
00:10:23,561 --> 00:10:26,520
the ases were there, but nothing very complicated.

183
00:10:26,521 --> 00:10:28,980
You have to realize that this was a labor force that very often

184
00:10:28,981 --> 00:10:30,180
had very little training.

185
00:10:30,320 --> 00:10:32,900
Shipwrights themselves, there were some people who built ships

186
00:10:32,901 --> 00:10:34,700
that were, in fact, extremely well-trained,

187
00:10:34,860 --> 00:10:36,960
but they often worked with a lot of people who weren't.

188
00:10:37,280 --> 00:10:38,340
You had to keep things very simple.

189
00:10:39,020 --> 00:10:40,580
And I think sometimes we have a tendency,

190
00:10:40,920 --> 00:10:42,360
because of the world we live in,

191
00:10:42,361 --> 00:10:44,680
to think that only complexity will get results.

192
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:45,980
Well, that's not the case.

193
00:10:46,740 --> 00:10:48,580
By the end of the 18th century,

194
00:10:49,240 --> 00:10:51,320
trade began to fuel industrialization,

195
00:10:51,840 --> 00:10:54,320
and the goods being carried in the waterways

196
00:10:54,321 --> 00:10:58,160
on which they traveled began to change ship design.

197
00:10:58,161 --> 00:11:01,320
Growing nations realized their prosperity was tied to the ships

198
00:11:01,321 --> 00:11:05,160
they sailed, and it fell to the shipyards and craftspeople

199
00:11:05,161 --> 00:11:08,880
to help build that future.

200
00:11:08,881 --> 00:11:11,360
During the 17th century, the British

201
00:11:11,361 --> 00:11:14,260
were one of the world's leading shipbuilders.

202
00:11:14,261 --> 00:11:17,060
Both an island nation and world power,

203
00:11:17,061 --> 00:11:20,220
Britain depended on its navy for international trade

204
00:11:20,221 --> 00:11:23,060
and to defend its vast holdings.

205
00:11:23,061 --> 00:11:25,960
Ships built in English port towns, such as Portsmouth,

206
00:11:25,961 --> 00:11:28,460
were constructed and repaired in some of the best shipyards

207
00:11:28,461 --> 00:11:30,200
of the day.

208
00:11:30,201 --> 00:11:33,660
Soon after the creation of the Royal Navy in 1670,

209
00:11:33,661 --> 00:11:37,340
the British government built the world's first stone dry dock.

210
00:11:37,341 --> 00:11:39,560
Also known as a graving dock, it

211
00:11:39,561 --> 00:11:42,260
allowed the British to repair their large ships of the line

212
00:11:42,261 --> 00:11:44,660
in a permanent and dry facility.

213
00:11:44,661 --> 00:11:47,100
Those docks are still in existence today

214
00:11:47,101 --> 00:11:48,820
and are still being used.

215
00:11:48,821 --> 00:11:53,220
One actually is the permanent home of the HMS Victory.

216
00:11:53,221 --> 00:11:55,700
Roughly 50 years after its founding,

217
00:11:55,701 --> 00:11:58,560
the burgeoning United States commissioned the first American

218
00:11:58,561 --> 00:12:00,060
graving docks.

219
00:12:00,061 --> 00:12:05,180
In 1827, engineers began building two, one in Boston,

220
00:12:05,181 --> 00:12:08,800
the second on the Elizabeth River in Portsmouth, Virginia.

221
00:12:08,801 --> 00:12:11,180
The civil engineering feat was one of the greatest

222
00:12:11,181 --> 00:12:15,080
the fledgling nation had ever undertaken.

223
00:12:15,081 --> 00:12:19,320
To dig this hole in the ground in 1827

224
00:12:19,321 --> 00:12:21,380
was an extraordinary event.

225
00:12:21,381 --> 00:12:26,080
They had to excavate this hole that was 400 feet long,

226
00:12:26,081 --> 00:12:28,780
approximately 20 feet deep at least.

227
00:12:28,781 --> 00:12:32,040
Then below that, they had to drive the piles down.

228
00:12:32,041 --> 00:12:33,940
Piles were like telephone poles.

229
00:12:33,941 --> 00:12:38,920
On top of these piles, then, they laid cut granite stone.

230
00:12:38,921 --> 00:12:41,980
For early shipyards that specialized in repair,

231
00:12:41,981 --> 00:12:45,620
The dry dock was their single greatest physical asset.

232
00:12:45,621 --> 00:12:49,060
But other American yards in the business of building new ships

233
00:12:49,061 --> 00:12:51,980
relied more on a form of shipyard natural selection

234
00:12:51,981 --> 00:12:53,560
for success.

235
00:12:53,561 --> 00:12:56,500
The history of shipbuilding in the United States

236
00:12:56,501 --> 00:13:00,660
goes back to the colonial time, because the East Coast

237
00:13:00,661 --> 00:13:04,260
was fortunate in having many small protected harbors,

238
00:13:04,261 --> 00:13:06,740
tidal areas where wooden ships could be built,

239
00:13:06,741 --> 00:13:11,100
and a great store of timber standing right nearby

240
00:13:11,101 --> 00:13:12,760
that could be used to build these ships.

241
00:13:15,640 --> 00:13:20,040
In the US from 1820 to 1890, more ships were built in Maine

242
00:13:20,041 --> 00:13:22,940
than anywhere in the United States.

243
00:13:22,941 --> 00:13:25,440
And due to unique geographic conditions,

244
00:13:25,441 --> 00:13:26,940
a majority of that building was done

245
00:13:26,941 --> 00:13:28,960
near the small town of Bath.

246
00:13:28,961 --> 00:13:31,380
The reasons for it included the availability

247
00:13:31,381 --> 00:13:34,720
of natural resources, a skilled labor pool,

248
00:13:34,721 --> 00:13:38,060
and as importantly, a long stretch of the river

249
00:13:38,061 --> 00:13:41,760
from Hospital Point to Day's Ferry, three miles,

250
00:13:41,860 --> 00:13:44,800
where the western shore, the Bath side of the river,

251
00:13:44,900 --> 00:13:46,860
was far enough away from the river

252
00:13:46,960 --> 00:13:49,700
to accommodate the length of the vessels being built.

253
00:13:49,800 --> 00:13:52,200
Vessels couldn't be built on the opposite shore,

254
00:13:52,300 --> 00:13:55,720
the Arousic shore, because of how steep the shore rises up

255
00:13:55,820 --> 00:13:57,820
out of the river.

256
00:13:57,920 --> 00:14:00,120
The coastline around Bath allowed building sites

257
00:14:00,220 --> 00:14:02,120
such as the Persean small shipyard

258
00:14:02,220 --> 00:14:05,560
to create mammoth wooden vessels, some of the biggest

259
00:14:05,660 --> 00:14:07,620
to ever take to the waves.

260
00:14:07,621 --> 00:14:10,020
One of the most important ingredients of a shipbuilding

261
00:14:10,120 --> 00:14:13,120
site like this here at Percy and Smalls Southways

262
00:14:13,220 --> 00:14:16,060
was finding an area, first of all, big enough to build the vessel

263
00:14:16,160 --> 00:14:17,760
one wanted to build, and secondly,

264
00:14:17,860 --> 00:14:20,260
one that had just the right slope to the land.

265
00:14:20,360 --> 00:14:23,660
It's hard to see, but this land here slopes about 3 quarters

266
00:14:23,760 --> 00:14:27,180
of an inch to a foot down to the river just right to launch the

267
00:14:27,280 --> 00:14:29,880
vessels that were built here.

268
00:14:29,980 --> 00:14:31,380
The launching of a ship had changed

269
00:14:31,480 --> 00:14:33,520
little since antiquity.

270
00:14:33,620 --> 00:14:37,620
Workers built the vessels on top of wooden rails known as ways.

271
00:14:37,621 --> 00:14:42,000
This is a model we use to demonstrate launching the completed wooden vessel.

272
00:14:42,800 --> 00:14:47,620
It would have been built right here in its position on top of the tracks or launching ways,

273
00:14:48,160 --> 00:14:54,600
but shortly before launching, a cradle would have been constructed on top of the ways between it and the bottom of the vessel.

274
00:14:55,240 --> 00:15:01,520
Just before launching, the two halves of the cradle would be snugged up against the base of the hull

275
00:15:01,521 --> 00:15:09,220
by wedges driven in between the two halves of the cradle.

276
00:15:09,840 --> 00:15:11,740
And then at just the right moment,

277
00:15:12,380 --> 00:15:14,100
at precisely slack high water,

278
00:15:14,180 --> 00:15:15,780
when there's no movement in the river at all,

279
00:15:16,340 --> 00:15:19,200
the keel blocks would start being knocked out

280
00:15:19,201 --> 00:15:20,200
from underneath the keel.

281
00:15:20,540 --> 00:15:23,080
And finally, at just the right moment,

282
00:15:23,580 --> 00:15:29,360
the vessel would slide, hopefully, into the river.

283
00:15:31,520 --> 00:15:34,420
The men who split out the blocks from under the tons

284
00:15:34,520 --> 00:15:37,420
of teetering ship had one of the most dangerous jobs

285
00:15:37,520 --> 00:15:40,720
in the yard, but they also had an upfront seat

286
00:15:40,820 --> 00:15:43,460
for the shipyard's most awesome spectacle.

287
00:15:43,560 --> 00:15:45,960
If one could have been standing here then

288
00:15:46,060 --> 00:15:48,380
and looking behind me and watching one of these big vessels

289
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:50,780
take shape, ultimately at the right moment,

290
00:15:50,880 --> 00:15:53,780
one would have watched it smoke past us with great thunder

291
00:15:53,880 --> 00:15:56,580
and applause and smoking timbers as it took

292
00:15:56,680 --> 00:15:57,780
to the Kennebec River.

293
00:15:57,781 --> 00:16:02,580
Cannebec River. Besides a near-perfect natural

294
00:16:02,581 --> 00:16:07,420
environment, Maine had another shipbuilding asset, its people.

295
00:16:07,421 --> 00:16:10,540
19th century shipyards were less of a physical establishment

296
00:16:10,541 --> 00:16:14,620
than a local gathering place. As had always been the case,

297
00:16:14,621 --> 00:16:17,820
whole communities were part of the shipbuilding process.

298
00:16:17,821 --> 00:16:21,520
In communities where there were many shipbuilding

299
00:16:21,521 --> 00:16:25,880
facilities, there were often tradesmen who set up shops that

300
00:16:25,881 --> 00:16:28,820
supplied services to many different shipyards.

301
00:16:28,821 --> 00:16:32,320
For example, a painter might go to paint vessels

302
00:16:32,321 --> 00:16:33,860
at various shipyards.

303
00:16:33,861 --> 00:16:37,520
A ship carver might produce lots of ship carvings,

304
00:16:37,521 --> 00:16:40,000
figureheads, trail boards, other carvings

305
00:16:40,001 --> 00:16:43,060
to decorate vessels built in many different shipyards.

306
00:16:43,061 --> 00:16:47,200
This is basically from the beginning of time

307
00:16:47,201 --> 00:16:51,540
to the end of wooden shipbuilding.

308
00:16:51,541 --> 00:16:55,540
But one change during the 19th century

309
00:16:55,640 --> 00:16:59,540
began the modern transformation of the shipyard, energy.

310
00:16:59,640 --> 00:17:03,080
As power is introduced, we see shipyards begin to take

311
00:17:03,180 --> 00:17:06,180
the physical form that we recognize as a shipyard today,

312
00:17:06,280 --> 00:17:10,100
space filled with shops for protecting the machinery that's

313
00:17:10,200 --> 00:17:13,300
used to produce the various parts of a vessel.

314
00:17:13,400 --> 00:17:15,860
First steam, and then electricity

315
00:17:15,960 --> 00:17:20,360
powered the tools and mechanized jobs once done entirely by hand.

316
00:17:20,361 --> 00:17:23,960
such as the cutting of wood and timber for the vessels.

317
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,600
All of the heavy wood that was shaped here at the mill

318
00:17:26,640 --> 00:17:29,160
was done with the aid of heavy machinery,

319
00:17:29,200 --> 00:17:33,840
most of which was run from a belt and shaft system

320
00:17:33,880 --> 00:17:37,080
that came across the top of the mill building.

321
00:17:37,120 --> 00:17:39,480
This machine for shaping heavy timbers

322
00:17:39,520 --> 00:17:41,040
was known as the Daniels Planer.

323
00:17:41,080 --> 00:17:43,680
It had a circular cutting head that would go around

324
00:17:43,720 --> 00:17:45,880
at a great rate of speed and could take up off

325
00:17:45,920 --> 00:17:48,280
to two inches at a time.

326
00:17:48,281 --> 00:17:52,280
the chips flew with such force that they actually gouged out

327
00:17:52,320 --> 00:17:56,280
areas of the ceiling and walls of the mill all around them.

328
00:17:56,320 --> 00:17:59,920
But all in all, it was a fairly effective tool for cutting down

329
00:17:59,960 --> 00:18:03,320
very, very thick and heavy pieces of timber.

330
00:18:03,360 --> 00:18:06,840
The shipyard's ability to use power tools to shape and trim

331
00:18:06,860 --> 00:18:10,840
the lumber allowed for greater efficiency in shipbuilding.

332
00:18:10,860 --> 00:18:14,280
Other trades began to appear within the yard.

333
00:18:14,300 --> 00:18:18,280
Workers once hired on a per-ship basis,

334
00:18:18,281 --> 00:18:22,520
found it more economical to set up shop right on the premises.

335
00:18:22,521 --> 00:18:25,580
Permanent design buildings, such as mold lofts,

336
00:18:25,581 --> 00:18:28,340
became part of the shipyard complex.

337
00:18:28,341 --> 00:18:30,780
There were literally dozens of different trades

338
00:18:30,781 --> 00:18:32,780
that went into the making of a wooden ship.

339
00:18:32,781 --> 00:18:35,080
And in a 19th century shipyard like this,

340
00:18:35,081 --> 00:18:36,680
it all began here in the mold loft,

341
00:18:36,681 --> 00:18:39,520
where the design started to take place.

342
00:18:39,521 --> 00:18:43,620
The designers would come up with a half model of the vessel,

343
00:18:43,621 --> 00:18:46,240
take its measurements, and blow them up full size here

344
00:18:46,241 --> 00:18:47,980
on the mold loft floor.

345
00:18:47,981 --> 00:18:50,940
That was in order to create patterns that could then be used

346
00:18:50,941 --> 00:18:54,320
to cut out the right-shaped timbers to build the schooners.

347
00:18:55,580 --> 00:18:58,920
Every single piece of wood that went into the frame of the schooner

348
00:18:58,921 --> 00:19:00,300
had to be sketched out first.

349
00:19:00,840 --> 00:19:03,380
That's why the mold loft became such an integral part

350
00:19:03,381 --> 00:19:05,420
of a shipyard like Percy and Small.

351
00:19:07,480 --> 00:19:09,540
Carpenters, overseen by master builders,

352
00:19:09,820 --> 00:19:12,380
then began the arduous task of assembling the timbers

353
00:19:12,381 --> 00:19:14,100
that eventually became the ship's hull.

354
00:19:14,101 --> 00:19:18,100
Within the shipyard, workers constructed the frames

355
00:19:18,101 --> 00:19:21,100
on a platform attached to the keel.

356
00:19:21,101 --> 00:19:25,100
When a frame piece was complete, oxen or horses pulled the frame

357
00:19:25,101 --> 00:19:28,100
down to the emerging hull, where it was raised into position

358
00:19:28,101 --> 00:19:32,100
by animal power, using block and tackle.

359
00:19:32,101 --> 00:19:34,100
Countless tradespeople, in turn, added their skills

360
00:19:34,101 --> 00:19:37,100
to the growing vessel.

361
00:19:37,101 --> 00:19:40,100
Everyone from plankers and caulkers to sailmakers

362
00:19:40,101 --> 00:19:43,100
were necessary in the 19th century shipyard.

363
00:19:43,101 --> 00:19:46,500
But the greatest change for shipyards and builders came

364
00:19:46,600 --> 00:19:48,500
with the crossover from wood to metal

365
00:19:48,600 --> 00:19:51,500
in the late 19th century.

366
00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:54,540
Hewing of timber and sewing of sails,

367
00:19:54,640 --> 00:19:56,740
jobs that had been performed for millennia,

368
00:19:56,840 --> 00:20:00,160
would all but disappear as the world's oceans

369
00:20:00,260 --> 00:20:04,360
began to be traversed by floating steel.

370
00:20:04,460 --> 00:20:07,200
Riveters, torchmen, and ironworkers

371
00:20:07,300 --> 00:20:10,860
began to populate the yards, each with their own tools

372
00:20:10,960 --> 00:20:13,100
and crafts.

373
00:20:13,101 --> 00:20:16,000
When you're dealing with riveted construction and steel,

374
00:20:16,100 --> 00:20:18,940
obviously the weight or displacement of these ships

375
00:20:19,040 --> 00:20:20,640
is going up dramatically.

376
00:20:20,740 --> 00:20:23,340
One of the key things they need to invent in the yards

377
00:20:23,440 --> 00:20:27,620
were cranes, something to pick up the heavy weight.

378
00:20:27,720 --> 00:20:30,220
They start out with steam-powered cranes that,

379
00:20:30,320 --> 00:20:31,720
really, they're called portal cranes

380
00:20:31,820 --> 00:20:33,160
that go around the side of the dry dock

381
00:20:33,260 --> 00:20:36,220
and have the large arms with the hook on the end

382
00:20:36,320 --> 00:20:37,720
that they can pick things up.

383
00:20:37,820 --> 00:20:43,100
And then they eventually shift it over from steam to electric.

384
00:20:43,101 --> 00:20:46,140
The powerful cranes came just in time to lift shipyards

385
00:20:46,240 --> 00:20:49,240
into a new century, a century that

386
00:20:49,340 --> 00:20:52,740
would see both the triumph of the massive steel vessels

387
00:20:52,840 --> 00:20:56,340
and the stunning destructive power unleashed by shipyards

388
00:20:56,440 --> 00:20:58,340
over the course of two world wars.

389
00:21:02,360 --> 00:21:04,820
Modern shipyards owed their very existence

390
00:21:04,920 --> 00:21:07,560
to the evolution of building materials and craftsmanship.

391
00:21:07,561 --> 00:21:11,560
ship.

392
00:21:11,660 --> 00:21:13,860
By the beginning of the 20th century,

393
00:21:13,960 --> 00:21:17,300
shipyards had undergone an epic change.

394
00:21:17,400 --> 00:21:19,300
The wooden ships that had brought the world closer

395
00:21:19,400 --> 00:21:23,640
together were now sailing into history.

396
00:21:23,740 --> 00:21:28,340
Industrialization and technology were replacing wood with steel.

397
00:21:28,440 --> 00:21:32,260
The age of the mammoth metal ship had begun.

398
00:21:32,360 --> 00:21:34,720
The thing about steel shipyards

399
00:21:34,721 --> 00:21:40,720
is that they do not come from wooden shipyards.

400
00:21:40,721 --> 00:21:43,960
The guys that have spent their lives

401
00:21:43,961 --> 00:21:46,600
building wooden sailing vessels are not

402
00:21:46,601 --> 00:21:49,800
going to wake up one morning and go to a different shipyard

403
00:21:49,801 --> 00:21:52,720
and be able to build steel vessels.

404
00:21:52,721 --> 00:21:57,480
The workforce for shipyards that build steel vessels

405
00:21:57,481 --> 00:22:03,360
comes from the foundries and the boilermakers.

406
00:22:03,361 --> 00:22:07,960
the new materials demanded precise work.

407
00:22:08,060 --> 00:22:11,760
Designers and naval architects began to be shipyard regulars

408
00:22:11,860 --> 00:22:14,740
as the old days of shaving wood with hand tools

409
00:22:14,840 --> 00:22:17,640
gave way to industrial presses that cut metal

410
00:22:17,740 --> 00:22:20,540
to exact specifications.

411
00:22:20,640 --> 00:22:22,480
In the United States, the technology

412
00:22:22,580 --> 00:22:24,580
began to change the yards, but it

413
00:22:24,680 --> 00:22:27,620
would take the First World War to unleash the nation's true

414
00:22:27,720 --> 00:22:29,960
shipbuilding potential.

415
00:22:29,961 --> 00:22:33,460
When World War I began in August 1914,

416
00:22:33,461 --> 00:22:37,460
the US had for years been depending on the ships

417
00:22:37,461 --> 00:22:40,680
of European nations, primarily England, Great Britain,

418
00:22:40,681 --> 00:22:45,440
but also France and Germany, to carry American commerce

419
00:22:45,441 --> 00:22:48,140
to the world.

420
00:22:48,141 --> 00:22:49,840
As foreign governments pressed their ships

421
00:22:49,841 --> 00:22:53,140
into military service, America needed to build

422
00:22:53,141 --> 00:22:56,380
overseas transportation.

423
00:22:56,381 --> 00:22:59,680
As a result, the US underwent a shipyard building boom.

424
00:23:05,680 --> 00:23:09,320
The foremost government project for shipbuilding

425
00:23:09,321 --> 00:23:12,960
in World War I was the installation at Hog Island,

426
00:23:12,961 --> 00:23:16,460
which is a kind of mudflats in the Delaware River

427
00:23:16,461 --> 00:23:18,600
near Philadelphia.

428
00:23:18,601 --> 00:23:21,800
And the government moved in with contractors.

429
00:23:21,801 --> 00:23:25,380
They're taking essentially an undeveloped space.

430
00:23:25,381 --> 00:23:29,080
You had to bring in all the machinery to move metal pieces

431
00:23:29,180 --> 00:23:32,880
to the shops to support, to fabricate.

432
00:23:32,980 --> 00:23:37,420
The Hog Island site cost over $65 million to build.

433
00:23:37,520 --> 00:23:39,960
For workers, the biggest job was stabilizing

434
00:23:40,060 --> 00:23:42,700
the barren, muddy land that sloped into the water.

435
00:23:45,760 --> 00:23:49,240
Steam-powered pile drivers pounded telephone pole-sized logs

436
00:23:49,340 --> 00:23:50,600
into the muck for support.

437
00:23:50,601 --> 00:23:56,100
The defining feature of the Mammoth construction site

438
00:23:56,101 --> 00:23:58,600
was the building of 50 shipways,

439
00:23:58,601 --> 00:24:02,600
able to accommodate vessels nearly 400 feet in length

440
00:24:02,601 --> 00:24:05,600
and over 50 feet wide.

441
00:24:05,601 --> 00:24:08,600
On either side of the shipways, permanent cranes

442
00:24:08,601 --> 00:24:11,600
were positioned to move the massive bulkheads

443
00:24:11,601 --> 00:24:14,600
and plate steel into position.

444
00:24:14,601 --> 00:24:18,600
At the height of production, over 30,000 shipyard workers

445
00:24:18,601 --> 00:24:22,940
workers manned the tools at Hog Island.

446
00:24:23,040 --> 00:24:25,500
But despite their best efforts, the shipyard

447
00:24:25,600 --> 00:24:28,740
had almost no impact on the war.

448
00:24:28,840 --> 00:24:31,240
The first ship built at the Hog Island facility

449
00:24:31,340 --> 00:24:35,860
didn't slide down the ways until August 5, 1918.

450
00:24:35,960 --> 00:24:38,560
Building a ship is a very complex process.

451
00:24:38,660 --> 00:24:44,500
A ship is the biggest movable thing that humans make.

452
00:24:44,600 --> 00:24:48,260
The logistics of that construction process

453
00:24:48,261 --> 00:24:51,960
are extremely important.

454
00:24:52,060 --> 00:24:54,360
The Yards designers had envisioned Hog Island

455
00:24:54,460 --> 00:24:58,000
as a fast-paced cargo ship assembly yard,

456
00:24:58,100 --> 00:25:02,600
where pre-built boilers, engines, propellers, and shaped

457
00:25:02,700 --> 00:25:06,020
steel arrived on train cars from manufacturers

458
00:25:06,120 --> 00:25:08,720
around the country.

459
00:25:08,820 --> 00:25:10,720
Unfortunately, the erratic supply stream

460
00:25:10,820 --> 00:25:13,120
and simultaneous site construction initially

461
00:25:13,220 --> 00:25:16,060
lowered the shipyard's efficiency.

462
00:25:16,061 --> 00:25:18,420
It took up to a year to complete a vessel.

463
00:25:22,360 --> 00:25:25,000
Cargo ships, the ones that were built for the war,

464
00:25:25,100 --> 00:25:27,900
were perfectly useful in commercial trade.

465
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:32,400
So here's this flood of ships now made available after 1920

466
00:25:32,500 --> 00:25:38,120
to the private operators, driving down the price of hulls,

467
00:25:38,220 --> 00:25:40,120
because there are so many of them competing

468
00:25:40,220 --> 00:25:41,760
for the small market.

469
00:25:41,860 --> 00:25:44,460
The shipyards can't produce new ships,

470
00:25:44,461 --> 00:25:48,460
And the industry was in depression before the Depression

471
00:25:48,560 --> 00:25:50,860
began in 1929.

472
00:25:50,960 --> 00:25:53,300
It went from bad to worse.

473
00:25:53,400 --> 00:25:55,100
Throughout the Great Depression,

474
00:25:55,200 --> 00:25:58,800
American shipyards closed at an alarming rate,

475
00:25:58,900 --> 00:26:00,900
while the ships built during the First World War

476
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:04,020
became more decrepit with each voyage.

477
00:26:04,120 --> 00:26:07,120
During the 1930s, President Roosevelt,

478
00:26:07,220 --> 00:26:10,260
frustrated by America's failing shipyard industry,

479
00:26:10,360 --> 00:26:12,560
created the US Maritime Commission

480
00:26:12,561 --> 00:26:16,160
to modernize the US merchant marine.

481
00:26:16,260 --> 00:26:19,300
The commission worked to design a standardized set of ships

482
00:26:19,400 --> 00:26:23,340
to carry US goods and oversaw the funding for shipyards

483
00:26:23,440 --> 00:26:25,840
to mass produce the new vessels.

484
00:26:25,940 --> 00:26:27,500
You go to the Maritime Administration

485
00:26:27,600 --> 00:26:29,920
and say, I'd like to build a new ship.

486
00:26:30,020 --> 00:26:31,240
They say, fine.

487
00:26:31,340 --> 00:26:32,920
We will find out what it would cost

488
00:26:33,020 --> 00:26:36,080
to build that ship at world rates, that is to say,

489
00:26:36,180 --> 00:26:41,720
at the lowest cost, adequate shipyard in the world.

490
00:26:41,721 --> 00:26:44,260
Then we'll find out how much it would cost to build it

491
00:26:44,261 --> 00:26:45,440
at a U.S. shipyard.

492
00:26:46,620 --> 00:26:47,720
We'll pay the difference.

493
00:26:48,760 --> 00:26:51,260
The subsidies program began to resuscitate

494
00:26:51,261 --> 00:26:52,320
the merchant shipyards.

495
00:26:54,200 --> 00:26:57,140
Meanwhile, Congress, fearing a second great conflict,

496
00:26:57,920 --> 00:27:00,000
recognized the need to build more warships,

497
00:27:00,560 --> 00:27:01,920
as well as repair facilities,

498
00:27:02,380 --> 00:27:04,460
to take care of the Navy's wide-ranging fleet.

499
00:27:05,200 --> 00:27:08,080
An explosion occurred really starting in 1938

500
00:27:08,081 --> 00:27:09,840
throughout the World War II period,

501
00:27:09,841 --> 00:27:13,680
but the period between 1942 and 1944,

502
00:27:13,780 --> 00:27:19,080
26 huge graving docks were put in service.

503
00:27:19,180 --> 00:27:21,480
In the entire previous history of the United States,

504
00:27:21,580 --> 00:27:24,480
only 29 graving docks have been put in service.

505
00:27:24,580 --> 00:27:28,300
And here in a period of two years, 26 graving docks.

506
00:27:28,400 --> 00:27:30,900
The new facility sprang up on both coasts,

507
00:27:31,000 --> 00:27:35,100
as well as Puerto Rico, Panama, and Hawaii.

508
00:27:35,200 --> 00:27:36,740
Engineers designed the new docks

509
00:27:36,741 --> 00:27:39,780
with the mammoth warships of the day in mind,

510
00:27:39,880 --> 00:27:44,080
and built some to repair multiple vessels simultaneously.

511
00:27:44,180 --> 00:27:47,480
These were 1,092-foot long graving docks.

512
00:27:47,580 --> 00:27:49,080
They weren't 300 feet.

513
00:27:49,180 --> 00:27:51,780
These were for the big ships, for the carriers.

514
00:27:51,880 --> 00:27:54,400
These draw docks were way over 100 feet wide,

515
00:27:54,500 --> 00:27:57,400
about 150 feet wide and over 40 feet deep.

516
00:27:57,500 --> 00:27:59,900
And they need a lot of them.

517
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:01,940
New underwater concrete technology

518
00:28:02,040 --> 00:28:05,300
allowed workers to cut construction time on these new docks

519
00:28:05,301 --> 00:28:07,940
from years to months.

520
00:28:08,040 --> 00:28:10,140
The method is called the Tremie Method.

521
00:28:10,240 --> 00:28:13,400
And basically, it's a way of pouring concrete underwater.

522
00:28:13,500 --> 00:28:15,740
Before, he had to actually put cofferdams up,

523
00:28:15,840 --> 00:28:18,480
and pump all the water out, and make it dry

524
00:28:18,580 --> 00:28:19,780
before he could lay the concrete.

525
00:28:19,880 --> 00:28:22,580
But this was a way that he could actually dredge out

526
00:28:22,680 --> 00:28:26,120
the area for the graving dock, place the forms down

527
00:28:26,220 --> 00:28:29,420
underwater, then actually through pipes,

528
00:28:29,520 --> 00:28:33,260
lay concrete underwater.

529
00:28:33,360 --> 00:28:34,860
Two of the Navy's graving docks

530
00:28:34,861 --> 00:28:37,700
were put to the ultimate test during the event that thrust

531
00:28:37,800 --> 00:28:41,600
the US into war, the Japanese attack at Pearl Harbor.

532
00:28:46,400 --> 00:28:49,200
One of the errors that the Japanese made on December 7,

533
00:28:49,300 --> 00:28:52,620
they didn't totally destroy the shipyard, specifically

534
00:28:52,720 --> 00:28:53,720
the dry docks.

535
00:28:53,820 --> 00:28:56,420
They did bomb the ships that were in the dry docks.

536
00:28:56,520 --> 00:28:59,460
Pennsylvania, the Downs, the Cassin were in dry dock one.

537
00:28:59,560 --> 00:29:01,360
They were all terribly damaged.

538
00:29:01,460 --> 00:29:04,420
But they didn't destroy the dry dock.

539
00:29:04,421 --> 00:29:07,620
Within weeks after the attack, shipyard workers were able to

540
00:29:07,720 --> 00:29:10,720
put numerous ships, including three heavily damaged

541
00:29:10,820 --> 00:29:13,760
battleships, back into service.

542
00:29:13,860 --> 00:29:17,360
Two additional battleships, two badly damaged to repair,

543
00:29:17,460 --> 00:29:20,060
were stripped clean, and their equipment and weapons

544
00:29:20,160 --> 00:29:24,280
were installed in new hulls at the San Diego Naval Yard.

545
00:29:24,380 --> 00:29:28,820
There was 7,000 dry dockings during World War II

546
00:29:28,920 --> 00:29:32,620
utilizing all these dry docks, so you could see the importance

547
00:29:32,621 --> 00:29:35,260
of dry docks in the war effort really, I think,

548
00:29:35,360 --> 00:29:37,060
turned the tide during the Pacific War.

549
00:29:37,160 --> 00:29:38,560
It was one of the key factors that

550
00:29:38,660 --> 00:29:40,360
made these ships available out there

551
00:29:40,460 --> 00:29:42,260
was having these repair assets available.

552
00:29:45,360 --> 00:29:47,060
Some of the Navy's wartime repairs

553
00:29:47,160 --> 00:29:52,180
were performed in 142 newly built floating dry docks.

554
00:29:52,280 --> 00:29:55,240
Some were individual modules that could be towed almost

555
00:29:55,340 --> 00:29:57,520
anywhere.

556
00:29:57,620 --> 00:30:00,580
Part repair facility and part submarine,

557
00:30:00,581 --> 00:30:03,620
Once fitted together, yard workers could open valves,

558
00:30:03,720 --> 00:30:06,420
which flooded and submerged the structure.

559
00:30:06,520 --> 00:30:08,720
When the vessel was in place, water

560
00:30:08,820 --> 00:30:12,020
was pumped out of the dock, causing it to rise,

561
00:30:12,120 --> 00:30:15,320
leaving the ship high and dry.

562
00:30:15,420 --> 00:30:16,620
During the Second World War, repair

563
00:30:16,720 --> 00:30:19,240
became very important, especially for the Navy,

564
00:30:19,340 --> 00:30:20,240
for two reasons.

565
00:30:20,340 --> 00:30:21,840
One was to repair battle damage.

566
00:30:21,940 --> 00:30:25,940
But the other thing was that technology was changing so fast,

567
00:30:26,040 --> 00:30:29,540
for example, with radar and the electronic systems,

568
00:30:29,541 --> 00:30:32,840
that there was a tremendous amount of work that had to be

569
00:30:32,940 --> 00:30:35,340
done to keep the ships up to date so that the ships were

570
00:30:35,440 --> 00:30:38,980
technologically superior to our enemies.

571
00:30:39,080 --> 00:30:40,580
But while some shipyards labored

572
00:30:40,680 --> 00:30:43,680
to keep the fleet afloat, others were welding and lifting

573
00:30:43,780 --> 00:30:45,280
their way into the record books.

574
00:30:48,100 --> 00:30:51,600
The United States was in the middle of a shipbuilding boom,

575
00:30:51,700 --> 00:30:54,100
and the shipyards were being called on to back

576
00:30:54,200 --> 00:30:55,600
the nation's war at sea.

577
00:30:59,540 --> 00:31:02,880
The carnage and destruction of World War II brought a golden

578
00:31:02,980 --> 00:31:04,480
age to American shipyards.

579
00:31:07,480 --> 00:31:10,780
Over half a million workers, many of whom were women,

580
00:31:10,880 --> 00:31:15,280
produced over 5,000 vessels for the war effort.

581
00:31:15,380 --> 00:31:18,100
Military ships were a clear necessity.

582
00:31:18,200 --> 00:31:21,700
At peak production, one shipyard, the Bath Iron Works

583
00:31:21,800 --> 00:31:26,600
in Maine, was able to launch a new destroyer every 17 days.

584
00:31:26,601 --> 00:31:28,900
But the true pinnacle of wartime shipbuilding

585
00:31:29,000 --> 00:31:32,740
was reached with the over 440-foot-long cargo carriers,

586
00:31:32,840 --> 00:31:35,000
known as Liberty Ships.

587
00:31:35,100 --> 00:31:37,740
In World War II, when we were building the Liberty Ships,

588
00:31:37,840 --> 00:31:42,640
and every ship was the same, as they built more and more ships,

589
00:31:42,740 --> 00:31:44,760
the production workers got better and better

590
00:31:44,860 --> 00:31:47,480
because they found little ways that they could build the ship

591
00:31:47,580 --> 00:31:48,560
faster.

592
00:31:48,660 --> 00:31:51,760
By 1943, many yards were producing

593
00:31:51,860 --> 00:31:53,960
a Liberty Ship every month.

594
00:31:53,961 --> 00:31:57,260
One ship, the Robert E. Perry, was constructed in a record

595
00:31:57,360 --> 00:32:01,200
four and a half days. Upon hearing of the speed at which

596
00:32:01,300 --> 00:32:03,960
they were produced, Hitler reportedly stated,

597
00:32:04,060 --> 00:32:08,600
we have lost the war. The shipyards themselves

598
00:32:08,700 --> 00:32:12,520
were little changed. But inside, a revolution had taken

599
00:32:12,620 --> 00:32:15,720
place. Shipyard workers relied on new modular

600
00:32:15,820 --> 00:32:19,760
construction techniques, taking prefabricated pieces of ships

601
00:32:19,860 --> 00:32:23,160
and welding them together, as opposed to using the older

602
00:32:23,161 --> 00:32:28,060
to using the older, much slower riveting process.

603
00:32:28,061 --> 00:32:29,960
But part of the Liberty ship's success

604
00:32:29,961 --> 00:32:32,660
was due to the use of technology developed over half

605
00:32:32,661 --> 00:32:36,460
a century earlier. The technology utilized

606
00:32:36,461 --> 00:32:39,820
for the massive shipbuilding program in World War II

607
00:32:39,821 --> 00:32:44,120
was predominantly late 19th century propulsion technology.

608
00:32:44,121 --> 00:32:47,160
Also, the hull forms and the metal shaping

609
00:32:47,161 --> 00:32:51,900
were essentially late 19th century.

610
00:32:51,901 --> 00:32:57,700
Cutting edge technology often requires a steep learning curve.

611
00:32:57,701 --> 00:33:00,900
Yard workers familiar with tried and true old ship construction

612
00:33:00,901 --> 00:33:04,700
methods were able to assemble the vessels with relative ease

613
00:33:04,701 --> 00:33:07,660
and help win the war.

614
00:33:07,661 --> 00:33:10,100
But as with the end of World War I,

615
00:33:10,101 --> 00:33:13,140
when hostilities ceased, the heightened wartime production

616
00:33:13,141 --> 00:33:18,060
of cargo ships nearly devastated a portion of the industry.

617
00:33:18,061 --> 00:33:21,500
With this enormous surplus of cargo carrying ships

618
00:33:21,501 --> 00:33:24,900
At the end of World War II, the shipyards, again,

619
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:28,240
had practically no business building cargo ships.

620
00:33:28,340 --> 00:33:30,140
But they were busy with the Cold War

621
00:33:30,240 --> 00:33:32,640
building military ships, naval ships.

622
00:33:32,740 --> 00:33:35,240
And so the shipyards were very busy.

623
00:33:35,340 --> 00:33:40,960
And the United States remained fully armed in naval terms

624
00:33:41,060 --> 00:33:45,060
after World War II.

625
00:33:45,160 --> 00:33:47,300
After the war, existing shipyards

626
00:33:47,400 --> 00:33:51,500
were redesigned to build and maintain ever larger ships.

627
00:33:51,501 --> 00:33:54,740
Engineers relied heavily on the modular shipbuilding techniques

628
00:33:54,840 --> 00:33:56,740
pioneered during the war.

629
00:33:56,840 --> 00:34:01,240
But now the individual pieces were building-sized structures,

630
00:34:01,340 --> 00:34:05,740
being hoisted and lowered by massive overhead cranes.

631
00:34:05,840 --> 00:34:09,160
But there was a downside to building on an epic scale.

632
00:34:09,260 --> 00:34:13,160
When a mistake was made, it was usually disastrous.

633
00:34:13,161 --> 00:34:14,060
disastrous.

634
00:34:19,460 --> 00:34:22,040
The size and complexity of these new vessels

635
00:34:22,140 --> 00:34:25,000
caused shipbuilders to rethink one of the oldest shipyard

636
00:34:25,100 --> 00:34:25,800
traditions.

637
00:34:29,040 --> 00:34:31,420
Historically, shipyards built ships on waves.

638
00:34:35,480 --> 00:34:39,280
And the way was basically inclined so that I could use

639
00:34:39,380 --> 00:34:42,420
the force of gravity when I released the ship from its

640
00:34:42,421 --> 00:34:45,280
it's holding condition, it would slide into the water.

641
00:34:45,281 --> 00:34:47,700
Well, if you think about trying to build something that's

642
00:34:47,701 --> 00:34:49,800
level, it's very difficult to do it

643
00:34:49,801 --> 00:34:51,900
if it's already on an incline.

644
00:34:51,901 --> 00:34:54,960
So if you build basically a land level facility

645
00:34:54,961 --> 00:34:57,600
where everything is referenced to a flat surface,

646
00:34:57,601 --> 00:35:00,700
it's much easier to assemble everything.

647
00:35:00,701 --> 00:35:03,900
So in the 60s and the 70s, the shipyards

648
00:35:03,901 --> 00:35:06,780
have transitioned from these launching ways where the ship

649
00:35:06,781 --> 00:35:09,480
is going to go down at an angle into the water

650
00:35:09,481 --> 00:35:12,820
to vessels that I'm going to build on a flat surface

651
00:35:12,821 --> 00:35:14,720
and launch it.

652
00:35:14,721 --> 00:35:17,020
In many shipyards, dry docks became

653
00:35:17,021 --> 00:35:18,880
the prime building areas.

654
00:35:18,881 --> 00:35:22,720
But that wasn't always the case.

655
00:35:22,721 --> 00:35:24,500
Specialized rail and lift systems

656
00:35:24,501 --> 00:35:28,840
began to be incorporated in many yards.

657
00:35:28,841 --> 00:35:30,800
Like a locomotive switching yard,

658
00:35:30,801 --> 00:35:34,680
ships were built and repaired on side tracks.

659
00:35:34,681 --> 00:35:36,580
The ships were then towed by tractors

660
00:35:36,581 --> 00:35:40,080
onto a hydraulic central track and moved almost anywhere

661
00:35:40,081 --> 00:35:43,280
in the yard.

662
00:35:43,281 --> 00:35:45,080
The technology allowed the shipyards

663
00:35:45,081 --> 00:35:48,320
to keep pace with the changing business.

664
00:35:48,321 --> 00:35:52,760
In the 1960s, shipyards did something called jumboizing.

665
00:35:52,761 --> 00:35:55,300
I had ships from the Second World War

666
00:35:55,301 --> 00:35:58,460
that there were still good life left in the machinery,

667
00:35:58,461 --> 00:36:00,740
the propelling equipment of the ship.

668
00:36:00,741 --> 00:36:02,780
However, the cargo carrying capacity

669
00:36:02,781 --> 00:36:04,980
wasn't quite large enough.

670
00:36:04,981 --> 00:36:07,120
So we'd bring the ship into a shipyard,

671
00:36:07,121 --> 00:36:09,580
and we'd basically cut the machinery away

672
00:36:09,581 --> 00:36:13,420
from the cargo section, throw the cargo section away,

673
00:36:13,421 --> 00:36:16,520
and install a new cargo section that was larger

674
00:36:16,521 --> 00:36:19,220
that we had already built and was waiting for the ship to arrive.

675
00:36:22,040 --> 00:36:24,040
But some of the biggest changes for shipbuilders

676
00:36:24,041 --> 00:36:26,700
took place inside the ships.

677
00:36:26,701 --> 00:36:29,340
Within a decade after the war, the US Navy

678
00:36:29,341 --> 00:36:32,640
began to use the same atomic energy that had so chillingly

679
00:36:32,641 --> 00:36:34,840
and effectively ended the conflict

680
00:36:34,841 --> 00:36:38,380
to power its new breed of ships.

681
00:36:38,381 --> 00:36:41,480
The shipyards of the 1950s and 1960s

682
00:36:41,481 --> 00:36:43,480
took responsibility for the nuclear vessels.

683
00:36:46,680 --> 00:36:48,780
The nuclear yards obviously had things

684
00:36:48,781 --> 00:36:50,760
they had to worry about that the others may not

685
00:36:50,761 --> 00:36:51,600
have to worry about.

686
00:36:51,601 --> 00:36:54,800
But specific craftsmen required people

687
00:36:54,801 --> 00:36:56,000
that were familiar with this type of work.

688
00:36:56,001 --> 00:36:58,740
It took, of course, increased training,

689
00:36:58,741 --> 00:37:02,100
and it took some special equipment that was available

690
00:37:02,101 --> 00:37:04,500
and additional security and that type of thing.

691
00:37:04,600 --> 00:37:07,600
But I think you'll find that all the Naval shipyards

692
00:37:07,700 --> 00:37:12,740
had a similar type of set of skills and similar shop names

693
00:37:12,840 --> 00:37:15,440
and similar tools.

694
00:37:15,540 --> 00:37:18,140
America was now building some of the best warships

695
00:37:18,240 --> 00:37:19,860
the world had ever seen.

696
00:37:19,960 --> 00:37:22,460
And a handful of shipyards were pushing technology

697
00:37:22,560 --> 00:37:25,060
to the limits.

698
00:37:25,160 --> 00:37:28,460
But the Navy's triumphs belied a serious problem.

699
00:37:28,560 --> 00:37:31,600
US shipbuilding was on a steep decline.

700
00:37:31,601 --> 00:37:34,540
And over the next few decades, conspiring forces

701
00:37:34,640 --> 00:37:40,300
would push American shipyards to near extinction.

702
00:37:40,400 --> 00:37:42,980
At the start of the 1980s, America's shipyards

703
00:37:43,080 --> 00:37:45,240
were entering a period of production not seen

704
00:37:45,340 --> 00:37:48,420
since the Second World War.

705
00:37:48,520 --> 00:37:51,360
The Cold War had led to increases in both the US

706
00:37:51,460 --> 00:37:53,320
and Soviet navies.

707
00:37:53,420 --> 00:37:57,900
And America was about to raise the stakes yet again.

708
00:37:58,000 --> 00:38:00,700
In 1980, President Reagan came in.

709
00:38:00,701 --> 00:38:04,440
He said, the Russian Navy is challenging our Navy.

710
00:38:04,540 --> 00:38:06,800
We're going to have a 600-ship Navy.

711
00:38:06,900 --> 00:38:09,000
This threw an enormous amount of construction

712
00:38:09,100 --> 00:38:11,940
to the shipyards that built for the Navy.

713
00:38:15,140 --> 00:38:19,960
The new ships were some of the most sophisticated ever built.

714
00:38:20,060 --> 00:38:22,060
Engineers began to rely on computers

715
00:38:22,160 --> 00:38:25,160
to both design and build the extremely complex vessels.

716
00:38:28,060 --> 00:38:30,200
Today, the computer's workhorse cousin,

717
00:38:30,201 --> 00:38:33,900
The robot lives on shop floors at places like the Northrop

718
00:38:34,000 --> 00:38:35,500
Grumman Newport News Shipyard.

719
00:38:40,000 --> 00:38:45,240
This is the six-axis ABB fully programmable robot.

720
00:38:45,340 --> 00:38:48,560
Its articulated arm moves similarly to a human's,

721
00:38:48,660 --> 00:38:51,360
but the welding torch at the tip does most of the work.

722
00:38:55,260 --> 00:38:58,700
Engineers download schematics directly into the machine,

723
00:38:58,701 --> 00:39:01,700
which then quickly welds the complex but often repetitive

724
00:39:01,701 --> 00:39:02,700
shapes.

725
00:39:06,700 --> 00:39:09,700
Machines like this have allowed shipyard builders to continue

726
00:39:09,701 --> 00:39:12,700
perfecting the efficient technique that originated during

727
00:39:12,701 --> 00:39:17,700
the First World War, modular construction.

728
00:39:17,701 --> 00:39:20,700
More and more, the interior portions of the ship,

729
00:39:20,701 --> 00:39:23,700
such as the engines, come in modules.

730
00:39:23,701 --> 00:39:25,700
The engine's already been tested at the factory.

731
00:39:25,701 --> 00:39:27,700
You already know it works.

732
00:39:27,701 --> 00:39:32,400
The radar system has been fully assembled and put together in New

733
00:39:32,401 --> 00:39:34,300
Jersey and tested.

734
00:39:34,660 --> 00:39:36,800
And then as a unit, you know it works.

735
00:39:36,880 --> 00:39:39,400
Then it's crated and shipped off to the shipyards.

736
00:39:41,880 --> 00:39:45,180
Today's shipyards are constantly fine-tuning their operations

737
00:39:45,181 --> 00:39:48,460
to maximize efficiency and maintain profits.

738
00:39:50,040 --> 00:39:53,120
The shipyard of the immediate future is going to look a lot like

739
00:39:53,121 --> 00:39:54,180
the shipyard of today.

740
00:39:54,760 --> 00:39:57,580
We're still going to build ships out of steel because steel is

741
00:39:57,581 --> 00:39:59,820
and it's relatively inexpensive.

742
00:39:59,920 --> 00:40:01,880
We're going to use welding technology.

743
00:40:01,980 --> 00:40:05,220
The real improvements in shipbuilding

744
00:40:05,320 --> 00:40:09,620
are going to come from the ability to plan and to integrate,

745
00:40:09,720 --> 00:40:12,820
that I will be able to take design information.

746
00:40:12,920 --> 00:40:15,240
That information will go to every vendor,

747
00:40:15,340 --> 00:40:18,240
and the vendors will know instantly everything that

748
00:40:18,340 --> 00:40:20,740
changes and how it affects them.

749
00:40:20,840 --> 00:40:22,280
But the shipyards of the future

750
00:40:22,380 --> 00:40:24,380
will depend on more than the savings brought on

751
00:40:24,480 --> 00:40:26,280
by proper planning.

752
00:40:26,281 --> 00:40:30,280
Throughout the 1980s, some shipyards, such as Newport News

753
00:40:30,380 --> 00:40:33,280
and the Bath Iron Works, thrived on substantial government

754
00:40:33,380 --> 00:40:36,280
orders for high-tech Navy ships.

755
00:40:36,380 --> 00:40:40,280
But Washington's actions nearly destroyed the rest of the industry

756
00:40:40,380 --> 00:40:42,880
by cutting subsidies that had kept the merchant shipbuilders

757
00:40:42,980 --> 00:40:45,880
afloat since the Great Depression.

758
00:40:45,980 --> 00:40:49,280
The loss meant merchant ship owners now paid more for ships

759
00:40:49,380 --> 00:40:52,780
built in America, and predictably, they looked overseas

760
00:40:52,880 --> 00:40:55,780
for cheaper, more efficient yards.

761
00:40:55,781 --> 00:41:02,780
At the end of the Second World War, the Japanese and the Western Europeans had a great advantage

762
00:41:02,781 --> 00:41:08,780
over the United States because many of them were in the position that they could rebuild their shipyards.

763
00:41:08,781 --> 00:41:12,780
So basically they were starting with what you might think of as a clean sheet of paper

764
00:41:12,781 --> 00:41:19,780
so that they could lay out everything in the most advantageous way to minimize the cost of production.

765
00:41:19,781 --> 00:41:24,280
For example, it takes about five times the number of movements

766
00:41:24,380 --> 00:41:28,780
in a US shipyard, all of which survived the Second World War,

767
00:41:28,880 --> 00:41:32,220
as it does in a Southeast Asian shipyard.

768
00:41:32,320 --> 00:41:34,720
The one-two punch of lost subsidies

769
00:41:34,820 --> 00:41:37,940
and more efficient foreign yards have had a profound effect

770
00:41:38,040 --> 00:41:40,740
on American shipyards.

771
00:41:40,840 --> 00:41:43,880
When I graduated from the Naval Academy in 1980

772
00:41:43,980 --> 00:41:45,440
with a Naval Architecture degree,

773
00:41:45,540 --> 00:41:47,840
there are 22 major shipyards in this country

774
00:41:47,841 --> 00:41:50,640
in this country involved in ship construction.

775
00:41:50,740 --> 00:41:52,840
Today, there are basically six.

776
00:41:52,940 --> 00:41:59,580
In 1982, we had about 112,000 people working in the industry.

777
00:41:59,680 --> 00:42:05,200
In 2002, production workers are about 45,000.

778
00:42:05,300 --> 00:42:08,000
As shipyards disappear from American shores,

779
00:42:08,100 --> 00:42:10,600
they have almost no chance of being rebuilt,

780
00:42:10,700 --> 00:42:12,940
since public facilities or housing tracts

781
00:42:13,040 --> 00:42:16,340
gobble up the valuable beachfront property.

782
00:42:16,341 --> 00:42:18,180
One of the largest shipyards in the United States,

783
00:42:18,181 --> 00:42:21,780
Hog Island, Pennsylvania, which was just south of Philadelphia,

784
00:42:21,781 --> 00:42:23,680
which was one of the most productive shipyards

785
00:42:23,681 --> 00:42:26,960
in the country in the 1920s, is now the Philadelphia

786
00:42:26,961 --> 00:42:29,960
International Airport.

787
00:42:29,961 --> 00:42:32,180
It would be very difficult to reclaim that back

788
00:42:32,181 --> 00:42:35,400
if you had to build a shipyard again.

789
00:42:35,401 --> 00:42:37,260
But the biggest loss will have little to do

790
00:42:37,261 --> 00:42:39,460
with machinery or dry docks.

791
00:42:39,461 --> 00:42:42,660
The biggest concern for those in the business today

792
00:42:42,661 --> 00:42:45,660
happens to the shipbuilders once the yards go away.

793
00:42:45,661 --> 00:42:48,660
There are some people that have been in those shipyards 20,

794
00:42:48,661 --> 00:42:52,660
30, 40 years that are living encyclopedias on how you do

795
00:42:52,661 --> 00:42:55,660
things and how you do them right.

796
00:42:55,661 --> 00:42:58,660
And they've seen what can go wrong.

797
00:42:58,661 --> 00:43:01,660
And when you lose that experience, you can't just pick

798
00:43:01,661 --> 00:43:03,660
it up off the street again.

799
00:43:03,661 --> 00:43:06,660
It takes 30, 40 years to build.

800
00:43:06,661 --> 00:43:09,660
Shipyards remain the places where builders use the world's

801
00:43:09,661 --> 00:43:13,960
use the world's largest tools to fashion the world's largest

802
00:43:13,961 --> 00:43:15,560
machines.

803
00:43:15,561 --> 00:43:19,300
But the loss of a shipyard is the loss of the age-old transfer

804
00:43:19,301 --> 00:43:22,560
of knowledge from master to apprentice.

805
00:43:22,561 --> 00:43:25,260
And that knowledge, once departed,

806
00:43:25,261 --> 00:43:28,560
may never find its way back to shore.

